# Constants Over String Enums and Unions

For project-owned finite string sets, define an `as const` object and derive the value type from it. Use this pattern instead of string enums and named unions of string literals.

```typescript
// Avoid
export type Name = "PRIMARY" | "SECONDARY";
export enum Status {
  Active = "ACTIVE",
  Inactive = "INACTIVE",
}

// Use
export const Name = {
  Primary: "PRIMARY",
  Secondary: "SECONDARY",
} as const;

export type Name = (typeof Name)[keyof typeof Name];
```

TypeScript keeps value and type names in separate namespaces, so use the same singular name for both. This preserves enum-like call sites such as `Name.Primary` and avoids separate singular/plural APIs.

Use the constant wherever a runtime value is needed instead of repeating the string literal.

Do not extract one-off string literal types that describe another API's keys or options, such as `Pick<T, "id" | "name">`, third-party component props, or framework configuration. Do not replace generated or dependency-owned enums.
